{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update12.json\", \"summary\": \"Validated (first attempt rejected for an undeclared investigation reference; added the investigation and revalidated → valid=true). Independent new direction: the production transport's heartbeat fast path, which deliberately runs a consensus handler on a connection goroutine. Recorded as a protective-mechanism negative rather than a defect. The admission test admits the fast path only for an AppendEntries request whose term is non-zero, whose leader address is present, and whose PrevLogEntry, PrevLogTerm, Entries and LeaderCommitIndex are all zero (net_transport.go:664-687), and only such a request is invoked directly on the connection goroutine instead of being handed to the consumer channel (net_transport.go:724-733). With those zeros, appendEntries cannot reach the previous-log comparison, the entry loop, or the commit block — the latter guarded by LeaderCommitIndex > 0 (raft.go:1565-1572) — so it cannot advance commit, apply logs or read the configuration off the main thread; what it does touch is atomic state/term plus mutex-protected leader and last-contact fields. The registration site states the requirement explicitly ('It MUST be safe for this to be called concurrently with a blocking RPC', api.go:613-616), and the zero-field admission test is what satisfies it — importantly, the commit-index-only re-replication sent on CommitTimeout does not qualify and therefore stays on the main thread. Recorded as one Fact, one active question with a discriminator (a race-detector run over a large-cluster snapshot catch-up would be the executed form; relaxing the admission condition would invalidate the argument), and an 'explained' interpretation with counterevidence and limits (NetworkTransport path only; in-memory transport stores the handler but does not fast-path it; no race detector was run). Two earlier suspicions were checked and discarded in this direction: file_snapshot.go's create/finalize ordering writes meta last with fsync and a crc64 state hash, and RecoverCluster's log replay skips configuration entries by design while the operator supplies the configuration.\"}"}}
